### How do you create a custom checked exception?

Extend `Exception` to create a checked exception. The compiler forces every caller to handle it.

```java
package com.bank.exception;

import java.io.Serial;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception {

    @Serial
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    private final BigDecimal balance;
    private final BigDecimal requested;

    public InsufficientFundsException(BigDecimal balance, BigDecimal requested) {
        super("Balance Rs." + balance + " insufficient for Rs." + requested);
        this.balance = balance;
        this.requested = requested;
    }

    public InsufficientFundsException(BigDecimal balance, BigDecimal requested, Throwable cause) {
        super("Balance Rs." + balance + " insufficient for Rs." + requested, cause);
        this.balance = balance;
        this.requested = requested;
    }

    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
    public BigDecimal getRequested() { return requested; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            throw new InsufficientFundsException(
                new BigDecimal("5000.00"), new BigDecimal("25000.00"));
        } catch (InsufficientFundsException e) {
            System.out.println("Checked: " + e.getMessage());
            System.out.println("Balance: Rs." + e.getBalance());
            System.out.println("Requested: Rs." + e.getRequested());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Checked: Balance Rs.5000.00 insufficient for Rs.25000.00
Balance: Rs.5000.00
Requested: Rs.25000.00
```

### How do you create a custom unchecked exception?

Extend `RuntimeException` for unchecked exceptions. The compiler does not force callers to handle them.

```java
package com.bank.exception;

import java.io.Serial;

public class InvalidTradeStateException extends RuntimeException {

    @Serial
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    private final String tradeId;
    private final String currentState;
    private final String expectedState;

    public InvalidTradeStateException(String tradeId, String currentState, String expectedState) {
        super("Trade " + tradeId + " in state " + currentState + ", expected " + expectedState);
        this.tradeId = tradeId;
        this.currentState = currentState;
        this.expectedState = expectedState;
    }

    public InvalidTradeStateException(String message, Throwable cause) {
        super(message, cause);
        this.tradeId = "UNKNOWN";
        this.currentState = "UNKNOWN";
        this.expectedState = "UNKNOWN";
    }

    public String getTradeId() { return tradeId; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            throw new InvalidTradeStateException("TRD-001", "SETTLED", "PENDING");
        } catch (InvalidTradeStateException e) {
            System.out.println("Unchecked: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Unchecked: Trade TRD-001 in state SETTLED, expected PENDING
```

### When should you use checked vs unchecked?

| Use Case | Checked (Exception) | Unchecked (RuntimeException) |
|---|---|---|
| Caller can recover | Yes: retry, fallback, prompt user | No: fix the code |
| Example | InsufficientFundsException | InvalidTradeStateException |
| Compiler enforces | Yes: try-catch or throws | No |
| Modern preference | Rare, truly recoverable only | Most cases |
| Spring convention | Wrapped in unchecked | Direct use |

### Why must you always preserve the cause?

Without the cause, the "Caused by:" chain is missing and debugging becomes guesswork.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class PreserveCauseDemo {

    // WRONG: cause lost
    static RuntimeException wrongWrap(Exception original) {
        return new RuntimeException("Settlement failed");
        // No cause. "Caused by:" section is GONE.
    }

    // CORRECT: cause preserved
    static RuntimeException correctWrap(Exception original) {
        return new RuntimeException("Settlement failed", original);
        // Cause preserved. Full chain visible.
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Exception original = new Exception("Deadlock on accounts table");

        RuntimeException wrong = wrongWrap(original);
        System.out.println("Wrong - cause: " + wrong.getCause());

        RuntimeException correct = correctWrap(original);
        System.out.println("Correct - cause: " + correct.getCause().getMessage());
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong - cause: null
Correct - cause: Deadlock on accounts table
```

### Why is swallowing exceptions the worst anti-pattern?

An empty catch block makes the exception disappear silently. In a banking context, this means money can be lost with zero evidence.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SwallowingAntiPatternDemo {

    // WORST: empty catch block
    public static void worst() {
        try {
            System.out.println("Debit SAV2001 Rs.50000: SUCCESS");
            throw new RuntimeException("Credit CUR3001 FAILED: DB timeout");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // NOTHING. Error disappears.
        }
        System.out.println("Method returns normally. Caller thinks everything worked.");
        System.out.println("Rs.50000 debited but never credited. LOST.\n");
    }

    // BAD: log but do not rethrow
    public static void bad() {
        try {
            throw new RuntimeException("Credit FAILED");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Logged: " + e.getMessage());
            // Logged but NOT rethrown. Caller thinks credit succeeded.
        }
        System.out.println("Caller still thinks operation succeeded.\n");
    }

    // CORRECT: log AND rethrow
    public static void correct() {
        try {
            throw new RuntimeException("Credit FAILED");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Logged: " + e.getMessage());
            throw new RuntimeException("Transfer failed: credit step", e);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== WORST: empty catch ===");
        worst();

        System.out.println("=== BAD: log only ===");
        bad();

        System.out.println("=== CORRECT: log + rethrow ===");
        try {
            correct();
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("Caller correctly sees failure: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
=== WORST: empty catch ===
Debit SAV2001 Rs.50000: SUCCESS
Method returns normally. Caller thinks everything worked.
Rs.50000 debited but never credited. LOST.

=== BAD: log only ===
Logged: Credit FAILED
Caller still thinks operation succeeded.

=== CORRECT: log + rethrow ===
Logged: Credit FAILED
Caller correctly sees failure: Transfer failed: credit step
```

### What constructors should a custom exception have?

Follow the standard four-constructor pattern from Throwable for maximum flexibility.

```java
package com.bank.exception;

import java.io.Serial;

public class TradeProcessingException extends RuntimeException {

    @Serial
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    public TradeProcessingException() {
        super();
    }

    public TradeProcessingException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }

    public TradeProcessingException(String message, Throwable cause) {
        super(message, cause);
    }

    public TradeProcessingException(Throwable cause) {
        super(cause);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TradeProcessingException ex = new TradeProcessingException(
            "Failed to process TRD-001",
            new RuntimeException("Connection refused"));
        System.out.println("Message: " + ex.getMessage());
        System.out.println("Cause: " + ex.getCause().getMessage());
    }
}
```

```text
Message: Failed to process TRD-001
Cause: Connection refused
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Exception hierarchy design.** Create a base `BankingServiceException` that all domain exceptions extend. This lets the global handler catch the base type as a fallback.
- **SonarQube rules.** `squid:S2142` flags empty catch blocks. `squid:S1166` flags missing cause in wrapping. Enable these in CI/CD.
- **Serialization.** Always include `serialVersionUID`. Without it, deserialization across different JVM versions may fail with InvalidClassException.
- **Immutability.** Make custom exception fields final. Exceptions should be immutable after construction.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Why include serialVersionUID? (For serialization compatibility across JVM versions.)
- Should custom exceptions be final? (Not necessarily, but make them if no subclassing is expected.)
- How do you design an exception hierarchy? (Base domain exception, then specific subtypes for each business error.)
- What is the modern preference? (Unchecked for most cases, global @ControllerAdvice handler.)
